---
// The privacy policy (GDPR Art. 13), kept as short as what the site does with data. It has
// to change in the same change as anything that collects or keeps more: a new form, a
// new provider, logins.
import TextPage from '../components/TextPage.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../lib/contact';

// Not a door date: the list outlives the last door by a few weeks, for the final email
// after the calendar and the Christmas break. Deleting it by then is a promise.
const LIST_DELETED_BY = 'January 31, 2027';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="privacy"
  description="What aocn.dev does with your data: your email address if you ask for door emails, and nothing else. No cookies, no tracking, only visit counts."
  pola={{ mood: 'neutral', message: 'I keep less about you than about the crew.' }}
>
  <TextPage command="$ cat privacy.txt" title="privacy">
    <p>Last updated: October 12, 2026</p>
    <p>
      This page explains what aocn.dev does with your data and what rights you have under the GDPR. The short version:{' '}
      <strong>your email address is stored only if you ask for door emails, and there are no cookies and no tracking. Visits are counted, but not who made them.</strong>
    </p>

    <h2>who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      Katharina Sick, Linz, Austria. aocn.dev is a personal project. For questions, requests or to have your data
      deleted, write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>door emails</h2>
    <p>If you sign up for door emails:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What is stored:</strong> your email address, when you signed up and when you confirmed.</li>
      <li><strong>Why:</strong> to send you one email whenever a door opens. Nothing else, and never to anyone else.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Legal basis:</strong> your consent (Art. 6(1)(a) GDPR). Signing up only sends a confirmation email; you
        are on the list once you click the link in it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Abuse protection:</strong> so that nobody can flood an inbox with confirmation emails, a keyed hash of
        the address (not the address itself) is kept for 24 hours, and requests are counted per IP address in memory.
        Legal basis: our legitimate interest in preventing abuse (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>visiting the site</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No cookies and no tracking. Fonts and scripts come from aocn.dev itself.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Visit counts:</strong> each page tells counter.dev that it was opened. When you arrive from another site,
        counter.dev also receives that site's address, and works out your country, browser, device type, operating
        system and language from the request. It keeps totals per day, plus a list of the last 30 visits (time, country,
        the site you came from, device type and operating system), never your IP address. Nothing is stored in or read
        from your browser for this. Legal basis: our legitimate interest in knowing how many people the calendar reaches
        (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR).
      </li>
      <li>Your browser remembers whether you switched the vim keys off. That setting never leaves your browser.</li>
      <li>
        The hosting providers log requests, including your IP address, to run and protect the service. Legal basis:
        legitimate interest (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>who processes your data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong><a href="https://resend.com/legal/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Resend</a></strong> sends the emails and keeps the list of subscribers.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://neon.com/privacy-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Neon</a></strong> hosts the database with the signup records, on servers in Frankfurt, Germany.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://cloud.google.com/terms/cloud-privacy-notice" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud</a></strong> runs the API, on servers in Belgium.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.netlify.com/privacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Netlify</a></strong> hosts the website.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each name links to that provider's own privacy policy. Each of them processes data only on our behalf, under a data processing agreement. All four are US companies, so
      data may be transferred to the USA; those transfers are covered by the EU-US Data Privacy Framework or the EU's
      standard contractual clauses.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong><a href="https://counter.dev/pages/privacy.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">counter.dev</a></strong> counts
      visits, without cookies. It is run from Germany, on servers at Linode in Frankfurt, behind Cloudflare.
    </p>

    <h2>how long it is kept</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The hash for abuse protection: 24 hours.</li>
      <li>
        Your email address and the record of your confirmation: until you ask for them to be deleted, and at the latest
        until {LIST_DELETED_BY}, when the whole list is deleted after the calendar's last emails.
      </li>
      <li>Request logs: as long as the hosting providers keep them, for Google Cloud 30 days.</li>
      <li>Daily visit totals: as long as counter.dev keeps them, which is indefinitely. The list of recent visits only ever holds the last 30.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask for a copy of your data, have it corrected or deleted, restrict or object to its processing, and get it
      in a machine-readable form. You can withdraw your consent to door emails at any time: write to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, or use the unsubscribe link in any door email. Withdrawing
      does not make earlier emails unlawful.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you think your data is processed unlawfully, you can complain to a data protection authority: in Austria the{' '}
      <a href="https://www.dsb.gv.at/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Datenschutzbehörde</a>, or the one in your country.
      Reaching out first is usually quicker.
    </p>

    <h2>changes</h2>
    <p>
      When the site starts doing more with data, for example once logins arrive, this page changes first, and the date
      at the top with it.
    </p>
  </TextPage>
</BaseLayout>
